<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-09-26T05:10:19Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Political psychology: Why extremist parties are thriving and what democratic politicians can do against it</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:529483c938cb1d7ed8cc5d2d7049d800dbde03004bb457ef1e07666918adb3cc</id><updated>2026-09-26T05:10:19Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T14:00:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/political-psychology-why-extremist-parties-are-thriving-and-what-democratic-politicians-can-do-again/a-79410960--440643795892.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/political-psychology-why-extremist-parties-are-thriving-and-what-democratic-politicians-can-do-against-it/a-79410960?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Far-right and far-left parties are celebrating success after success, leaving moderate politicians in the dust and worrying many Germans. Political psychology helps us understand what's behind growing polarization.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Could flattering AI make humanity turn on itself?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7437485f9695a03ebf32302056c701535708a8ed1907f3dcac8e3da06e5329c3</id><updated>2026-09-26T05:10:19Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T13:10:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/could-flattering-ai-make-humanity-turn-on-itself/a-79377894--285f77843c72.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/could-flattering-ai-make-humanity-turn-on-itself/a-79377894?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A study shows chatbots mirror users' politics, part of a broader tendency known as AI sycophancy. Researchers fear this could deepen polarization.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Male infertility is 'grossly underreported'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:93efcb13fc866aaf85f43a7c61c145372e43519880a971613c0b484c1d360a2e</id><updated>2026-09-26T05:10:19Z</updated><published>2026-09-22T10:00:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/male-infertility-is-grossly-underreported/a-79368722--14a06576edd4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/male-infertility-is-grossly-underreported/a-79368722?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">It's not all on women anymore — doctors do test men for sperm count and motility. But still too few men get checked, possibly due to a misconception that a healthy sex life means healthy sperm. Spoiler: It doesn't.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
